<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Mendès France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–<a href="/years/1982">1982</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre Mendès <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was a French Radical politician and prime minister from June 1954 to February 1955. After the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> he negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Accords that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>, and he opened the path to Tunisian autonomy. His brief government became a model of reforming leadership in the Fourth Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-mendes-france.jpg" alt="Pierre Mendès France" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Studio Harcourt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mend%C3%A8s-France_Harcourt_1948.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pierre Isaac Isidore Mendès France was born on 11 January 1907 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the son of a textile merchant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a> and a descendant of Portuguese Jews who settled in France in the 16th century. He joined the Radical Party in 1924, took a doctorate in law and in 1926, at nineteen, became the youngest member of the Paris bar. In 1932 he was elected to the Chamber of Deputies for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eure">Eure</a>, the youngest member of the Assembly, and in 1938 the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-blum">Léon Blum</a> appointed him Under Secretary of State for Finance.</p><p>In October 1940 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime tried him for desertion after he boarded the liner SS Massilia for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casablanca">Casablanca</a> to continue the fight, and imprisoned him. He escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and joined the Free French forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>, flying in a dozen bombing raids. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-of-paris">liberation of Paris</a> in August 1944 de Gaulle appointed him Minister for National Economy, but he resigned when de Gaulle sided with the Finance Minister, René Pleven, against his support for state regulation of wages and prices.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Mendès <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> failed to form a government in June 1953. After the French defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> in May 1954, the government of Joseph Laniel resigned on 12 June, and he formed a government with support from the centre-right. He negotiated at once with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a>, saying there was no choice but total withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>, and the Assembly backed him by 471 votes to 14. Nationalist opinion was shocked, and he was the target of abuse, much of it anti-Semitic.</p><p>On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, after the outbreak of the war in 1954, he declared that its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> were an integral part of the French Republic and that the idea of secession was unthinkable. He agreed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habib-bourguiba">Habib Bourguiba</a> on the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> by 1956 and began discussions with nationalist leaders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. He also helped bring about the Western European Union, but the National Assembly rejected the proposed European Defence Community. His cabinet fell in February 1955.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1956 he served as Minister of State under Guy Mollet but resigned over Mollet's handling of the Algerian War, and he resigned as Radical Party leader in 1957. He opposed de Gaulle's return to power in 1958, lost his seat that November, and later joined the Unified Socialist Party. He sat again in the Assembly in 1967 to 1968 and expressed sympathy for the student protesters of May 1968. In 1971 he supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand">François Mitterrand</a>'s new Socialist Party, and he died on 18 October 1982, having lived to see Mitterrand elected president.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pierre Mendès France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Defeat at Dien Bien Phu ended the Indochina war in 1954, and <b>Pierre Mendès France</b> negotiated the withdrawal.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-fourth-republic">French Fourth Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in 3rd arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in 16th arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.36&amp;year=1955" aria-label="3rd arrondissement of Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4605 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pierre Mendès France"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5055 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5055 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.36&amp;year=1955">What was here in 1955? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
